course: geography
lesson: 47
title: Famous eruptions and living with volcanoes
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some eruptions changed history: in AD 79 Vesuvius buried Pompeii and Herculaneum, and in 1815 Tambora threw so much ash and gas into the sky that the next year had no proper summer.
explain: A volcano's reach can be enormous: Krakatoa in 1883 was heard nearly 5,000 km away, Laki in 1783-84 poisoned the animals of Iceland, and in 2010 an Icelandic ash cloud stopped most flights in Europe for about a week.
explain: Yet millions of people live on volcanoes' slopes on purpose, because volcanic ash weathers into some of the richest farming soil on Earth, and the heat underground can warm homes and make electricity.
explain: Imagine a farmer near Naples whose tomatoes grow huge in dark volcanic soil, while instruments on Vesuvius above his farm listen day and night for the small earthquakes that come before an eruption.
explain: Volcanologists cannot give an exact date, but swelling ground, swarms of small quakes and changing gases often give enough warning to move people away.
fact: In AD 79 Vesuvius buried the Roman towns of Pompeii and Herculaneum under ash and pyroclastic flows.
fact: Pliny the Younger wrote an eyewitness account of the AD 79 eruption, and tall ash-column eruptions are now called Plinian.
fact: Tambora in Indonesia erupted in 1815, the largest eruption in recorded history, killing tens of thousands of people.
fact: After Tambora, its ash and gas high in the sky cooled the climate, and 1816 became the "year without a summer" in Europe and North America, with snow in June and failed harvests.
fact: Krakatoa's eruption in 1883 was heard nearly 5,000 km away, and its tsunamis killed over 36,000 people.
fact: The Laki eruption in Iceland in 1783-84 poured out poisonous gas that killed most of Iceland's livestock, and about a fifth of its people died in the famine that followed.
fact: Mount St Helens in the USA erupted in 1980 when its whole north side collapsed in a giant landslide and the blast burst out sideways.
fact: People live near volcanoes because volcanic ash weathers into very fertile soil.
fact: Iceland sits on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, with volcanic heat close to the surface, and it uses this heat to make electricity and to heat almost all its homes.
fact: Volcanologists watch for small earthquakes, swelling ground and changes in gas to warn when an eruption is coming.
fact: The 2010 eruption of Eyjafjallajökull in Iceland grounded most flights across Europe for about a week, because volcanic ash can melt inside jet engines and stop them.
exercise: What happened to Pompeii and Herculaneum in AD 79? | Vesuvius buried them under ash and pyroclastic flows | An earthquake sank them both into the sea in a single night | A flood from the river washed them away | An army from Carthage burned them to the ground
exercise: Who wrote an eyewitness account of the AD 79 eruption? | Pliny the Younger | Julius Caesar, the general | Alfred Wegener, the scientist | Christopher Columbus
exercise: What is a Plinian eruption? | An eruption with a tall column of ash, named after Pliny | An eruption of runny lava only, with no explosion | An eruption under the sea | An eruption that makes no ash at all, only gentle glowing rivers
exercise: Which eruption is the largest in recorded history? | Tambora, Indonesia, 1815 | Vesuvius, near Naples, Italy, AD 79 | Mount St Helens, USA, 1980 | Eyjafjallajökull, Iceland, 2010
exercise: Why was 1816 called the "year without a summer"? | Tambora's ash and gas cooled the climate, with snow in June | The Sun stopped shining for a month and the Earth went dark | A long war cancelled all the summer holidays across Europe | The calendar skipped the summer months
exercise: How far away was Krakatoa's eruption in 1883 heard? | Nearly 5,000 km away | About 5 km away, in the next town | About 50 km away, on nearby islands | Nobody heard it at all
exercise: What killed most of the victims of Krakatoa? | Tsunamis caused by the eruption | Lava flowing slowly into the cities | Cold weather the next winter and famine | Lightning from the giant ash cloud
exercise: What did the Laki eruption of 1783-84 do to Iceland? | Its poisonous gas killed most livestock and about a fifth of the people died in famine | It created a brand new island the size of Iceland itself | It heated every home for a hundred years | It pushed Iceland across the sea until it joined up with Greenland for good, making one big land
exercise: How did Mount St Helens erupt in 1980? | Its whole north side collapsed in a giant landslide | It poured out gentle lava slowly for ten years | It sank into the sea | It erupted only white steam, with no ash or rock at all
exercise: Why do people choose to live near volcanoes? | Volcanic ash weathers into very fertile soil | The air is always much cleaner and fresher there | Volcanoes keep earthquakes away from the area | Land near volcanoes is never flooded by rivers
exercise: How does Iceland use its volcanic heat? | To make electricity and heat almost all its homes | To melt its glaciers for drinking water | To cook food in open lava pools in the town squares | It doesn't use it at all, as it is far too dangerous
exercise: What do volcanologists watch for to warn of an eruption? | Small earthquakes, swelling ground and changes in gas | The phases of the Moon and the height of the tides | The colour of the sky at sunset | How many birds sit on the volcano and when they leave
exercise: What did the 2010 eruption of Eyjafjallajökull do? | It grounded most flights across Europe for about a week | It destroyed the city of Reykjavik and its harbour | It caused a tsunami that flooded the coast of Britain | It made a year without a summer
exercise: Why does volcanic ash stop planes flying? | It can melt inside jet engines and stop them | It makes the sky too dark for pilots to see the Moon | It weighs down the wings like snow | It confuses the pilots' radios and nothing else
test: A cartoon shows the people of Pompeii slowly drowned by a river of lava they could easily outwalk. What's closer to the truth? | Ash fall and fast pyroclastic flows buried the towns | They were all killed by a tsunami from the bay and nothing else | The town was just abandoned with no eruption | Cold lava froze them in ice
test: "snow in june?? in 1816?? why" | A volcano, Tambora, filled the sky with ash and gas that cooled the Earth | The Earth moved much further away from the Sun that year, so the whole planet got cold | A giant glacier slid into Europe | The Moon blocked the Sun all summer, a long eclipse lasting for months
test: Your mate says: "Only idiots live next to a volcano." What's the fairer answer? | Volcanic soil is rich for farming, and warnings can give time to leave | He's right, nobody gets anything good from volcanoes, only danger | Volcanoes never erupt where people live | People there are paid by the government to guard the volcano day and night
test: What would happen today if a Tambora-size eruption happened again? | Harvests could fail around the world for a year or more | Only Indonesia would notice anything at all | The Earth would get much warmer for a whole century | Nothing, modern cities have roofs that stop ash
test: A volcano's ground has swollen 50 cm and small quakes are happening every hour. What should the authorities do? | Prepare to move people away, an eruption may be coming | Wait until lava appears before doing anything | Tell everyone to climb up and look | Nothing, those signs mean the volcano is dying
test: "bloody volcano in iceland and my holiday flight's cancelled, what a joke" Why was it cancelled? | Volcanic ash can wreck jet engines, so flights are grounded | Iceland closed all the airports in the world for its own safety | The planes were needed to rescue people | The ash made the runways too hot
test: How could Laki in Iceland kill so many people without burying any town? | Its poisonous gas killed the livestock, and people starved | Its lava flowed across the whole country | It caused a giant tsunami | Its noise made everyone deaf
test: Mount St Helens erupted sideways, not straight up. Why? | Its north side collapsed in a landslide, letting the blast out sideways | It was hit by a strong wind that bent the column of ash right over | It erupted under a lake | Its top had been dug away by miners
test: A clever puzzle: Krakatoa's bang was heard nearly 5,000 km away. Sound travels about 1,200 km an hour. Roughly how long after the blast did the far listeners hear it? | About 4 hours | About 4 seconds, at once | About 4 days | About 4 minutes
test: Why is Iceland one of the few countries that heats almost all homes from the ground? | It sits on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, with volcanic heat close to the surface | It is closer to the Sun than any other country on Earth, so its rocks are warm | It burns all its forests for heat | It imports hot water from Africa in huge tanker ships every single month
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequences of the eruption of Tambora in 1815? | A climatic disturbance which rendered 1816 a year without a summer in Europe and North America | The destruction of the Roman towns of Pompeii and Herculaneum | The grounding of aviation throughout Europe for a period of one week | The collapse of the northern flank of a volcano in the United States
exam: Identify the principal reason for which human settlement persists in the vicinity of active volcanoes. | The exceptional fertility of soils derived from volcanic ash | The total absence of seismic activity in such regions of the world | The legal prohibition of settlement elsewhere | The protection which volcanoes afford against flooding
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the monitoring of volcanoes. | Seismic activity, deformation of the ground and changes in gas emission are observed | The phases of the Moon are recorded and the date of eruption is computed | The colour of the summit is photographed annually | No observation is possible prior to the emission of lava
exam: It is given that the eruption of Eyjafjallajökull occurred in 2010. Ascertain the consequence thereof which is consistent with the established facts. | The suspension of the majority of flights across Europe for approximately one week | The death of approximately one-fifth of the population of Iceland through famine and disease | The generation of tsunamis killing over thirty-six thousand persons in Indonesia | The burial of a Roman town beneath ash
